+      "title": "All normalised moment measures determine a random probability-measure limit",
+      "statement": "Let S be Polish and mu_n, mu be random Borel probability measures on S. If E[mu_n^{tensor r}] converges weakly to E[mu^{tensor r}] on S^r for every integer r>=1, then mu_n converges in law to mu in P(S) with the weak topology. First-moment convergence yields tightness of the random-measure laws by Markov bounds on compact complements. All mixed moments of bounded continuous integral vectors then determine their compactly supported laws. Every normalised r-th moment measure has total mass exactly one; no factorial-mass or Carleman argument is needed.",
+      "proof_text": "# C0075: normalised moment measures give a precise conditional reduction\n\nLabel: proof of a general random-probability-measure lemma and a refuted finite-moment route, pending w347 KEEP. No percolation scaling limit is claimed proved.\n\nLet S be Polish, let mu_n and mu be random Borel probability measures on S, and assume that for every integer r>=1 the deterministic probability measures E[mu_n^{tensor r}] converge weakly on S^r to E[mu^{tensor r}]. Then mu_n converges in distribution to mu in the space P(S) with its weak topology.\n\nProof of tightness: the first moment measures converge and hence form a uniformly tight family. Given epsilon>0, choose compact K_j with sup_n E[mu_n(K_j^c)] <= epsilon*2^(-2j), j>=1. Markov's inequality and a union bound show that with probability at least 1-epsilon, all inequalities mu_n(K_j^c)<=2^(-j) hold. The corresponding set A of measures is closed (K_j^c is open, so nu -> nu(K_j^c) is lower semicontinuous) and uniformly tight; Prokhorov makes A compact. Hence the laws of mu_n are tight.\n\nProof of uniqueness: any subsequential law limit has the same expectations of products of bounded continuous integrals nu(f_1)...nu(f_r) as mu, by the assumed moment-measure convergence. For a finite family of bounded f_i the integral vector lies in a compact box. Polynomial approximation on that box determines its distribution from all mixed moments. A countable family of bounded continuous functions determining the weak Borel structure on P(S) then determines the law of the random measure. Every subsequential limit therefore has law mu.\n\nThe normalisation matters. E[mu^{tensor r}](S^r)=1 for every r, not a factorially growing quantity. No Carleman bound is needed for these bounded evaluation vectors. Pointwise limits of characteristic functions of these probability moment measures imply their weak convergence if the limiting characteristic function is continuous at the origin; mass one alone is not a tightness proof (delta_n escapes on R).\n\nKnowing only the first few moment measures is insufficient. On S={0,1}, set mu_P=(1-P)delta_0+P delta_1. Compare P uniform on [0,1] with P supported on {0,1/2,1} of weights {1/6,2/3,1/6}. They have the same moments through degree 3, so the first three moment measures agree, but E P^4 is 1/5 versus 5/24. Their fourth moment measures, and their laws, differ.\n\nFor the critical-cluster empirical measure n^(-1) sum_{x in C(0)} delta_{n^(-1/4)x}, conditioned on |C(0)|=n, apply the lemma only after checking convergence of every normalised moment measure to the corresponding ISE moment measure. Verifying those lace-expansion asymptotics, particularly the nearest-neighbour regime and the correct scaling constants, remains the substantive gap. A theorem about two or three point functions cannot be promoted to the required all-order statement.\n\nSource: C0075 problem and attempt in the 2026-09-07 intake; cached Heydenreich-van der Hofstad Open Problem 15.1. The prior attempt's factorial mass and unverified all-order inference are not used.\n\nPrimary-source check: the cached survey section 15.1 expressly distinguishes the conditioned three-point Hara-Slade result from missing higher-point results in that source. Its reference [141] is T. Hara and G. Slade, The scaling limit of the incipient infinite cluster in high-dimensional percolation. II. Integrated super-Brownian excursion, J. Math. Phys. 41(3):1244-1293 (2000). With the origin fixed, a (k+1)-point cluster function corresponds to the k-th empirical moment measure after normalisation; this indexing must also be checked.\n",
