+      "title": "Attainable peak sets on finite connected graphs are precisely nonempty independent sets",
+      "statement": "For a finite nonempty connected simple graph, a vertex set S is exactly the set of strict local maxima of some bijective vertex labeling if and only if S is nonempty and independent. Assign the largest labels to S and assign the other labels in decreasing order of distance from S to construct such a labeling. Consequently any nonadjacent two vertices can be the only peaks. On n-by-n grids both graph distances 2 (n>=3) and 2(n-1) occur with positive probability conditioned on two peaks. This support statement does not determine whether distance/n converges to zero.",
+      "proof_text": "# P0029: attainable peak sets and exact finite-grid controls\n\nLabel: proof of a support lemma plus exact finite numerical evidence, pending w347 KEEP. The source asks whether distance/n tends to zero, despite its informal repel wording; we retain the displayed question.\n\nFor a finite connected graph G, a subset S is the exact set of strict local maxima of some bijective vertex labeling if and only if S is nonempty and independent. Necessity: the globally largest label is a local maximum, and two adjacent vertices cannot both exceed each other. Sufficiency: give S the |S| largest labels. Order the other vertices by nonincreasing distance from S, giving nearer vertices larger labels; resolve equal-distance ties arbitrarily. Every nonroot has a neighbour one step closer to S, hence a neighbour with a larger label. Every root has only nonroot neighbours and hence is a peak. There are no other peaks.\n\nConsequently any two nonadjacent prescribed vertices can be the two peaks with positive probability under a uniform random permutation. On square grids, both graph distance 2 (n>=3) and distance 2(n-1) are in the conditional support. Support alone cannot determine concentration or an asymptotic probability: rare configurations at macroscopic distance do not refute distance/n ->0, and close configurations do not prove it.\n\nExact permutation counts for the 2x2, 2x3 and 3x3 grids accompany this proof. They extend the prior 2x2 count of 8/24. They are finite controls and do not establish a limiting separation law. The remaining route is a quantitative bound on the weighted counts of acyclic orientations with two sinks (each orientation is weighted by its number of linear extensions), not merely existence of orientations.\n\nSource: existing P0029 open-problem and attempt cards, citing Burdzy, My favorite open problems, Problem 9.\n",
