+    {
+      "key": "susceptibility-value-comparison-does-not-control-log-derivative",
+      "namespace": "probability.percolation",
+      "logical_name": "susceptibility-value-comparison-does-not-control-log-derivative",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Uniform value comparison does not control a logarithmic-derivative maximiser",
+      "statement": "For V tending to infinity there are positive increasing C1 functions f_V and g_V on [0,1] with f_V<=g_V<=exp(0.1) f_V, for which the log derivative of f_V is maximised at 1/2 but that of g_V is outside every fixed K V^(-1/3) window around 1/2 for sufficiently large V. Set a=V^(1/3), f_V=a exp(arctan(a(p-1/2))) and g_V=f_V exp(S((p-3/4)V^2)/10), where S is the increasing cubic smoothstep from 0 to 1 with derivative 3/4 at 0. The added log derivative at 3/4 is (3/40)V^2. This is a calculus counterexample to a value-to-derivative inference, not a percolation susceptibility or a refutation of torus criticality.",
+      "proof_text": "# C0067: comparable susceptibility values do not locate the derivative maximum\n\nLabel: proof of a counterexample to a calculus inference, pending w347 KEEP. The constructed functions are not percolation susceptibilities; this does not refute Open Problem 13.1.\n\nFor V>=1000 set a=V^(1/3), p_c=1/2, p_0=3/4, h=V^(-2), and f_V(p)=a exp(arctan(a(p-p_c))). This positive increasing C1 function has logarithmic derivative a/(1+a^2(p-p_c)^2), maximised at p_c. Let S(t)=0 for t<=-1, S(t)=1 for t>=1, and S(t)=1/2+3t/4-t^3/4 between -1 and 1. This is C1 and increasing, with S'(0)=3/4. Define g_V(p)=f_V(p) exp(S((p-p_0)/h)/10).\n\nAt every point f_V<=g_V<=exp(1/10) f_V. Yet the logarithmic derivative of g_V at p_0 is a/(1+a^2/16)+(3/40)V^2, whereas throughout |p-p_c|<=V^(-1/3) the perturbation derivative is zero and the logarithmic derivative is at most a. The maximum is therefore outside this window. The same holds outside every fixed K V^(-1/3) window for sufficiently large V. The perturbation preserves monotonicity and value bounds while changing derivative location drastically.\n\nThus two-sided plateau bounds for susceptibility itself cannot by themselves establish the location of the maximum of its logarithmic derivative. A precise sufficient missing input is an interior lower bound for this derivative that strictly beats a uniform exterior upper bound. The source's actual percolation structure may prohibit the constructed narrow bump; proving such derivative or pivotal control is the work still required. This attempt concerns p_c^(1); no assertion about the other definitions follows from it.\n\nSource: C0067-A001, whose recorded obstruction is that two-sided derivative control is unavailable, and Heydenreich-van der Hofstad, Open Problem 13.1.\n",
