+      "statement": "Under a fair legal Z2 sandpile schedule with toppling threshold 4, every configuration at least 3 everywhere with a height-4 seed has every site topple infinitely often. A finite nonnegative odometer would be superharmonic everywhere and strictly so at the seed, contradicting recurrence. Infinite topplings then propagate to neighbours. Thus an IID law on {3,4} has infinite toppling probability zero if P(4)=0 and one if P(4)>0. For rationally specified probabilities, testing P(4)=0 gives a finite decision algorithm on this face; for arbitrary real inputs this is a probability criterion without a claimed computable zero test. Every law on {0,1,2,3} is already stable. In particular the stable background of height 3 plus one extra chip need not stabilise; a blanket stable-background-plus-finite-perturbation claim is false.",
+      "proof_text": "# C0098: a decidable {3,4} subfamily and a corrected finite-perturbation claim\n\nLabel: proofs of boundary cases, pending w347 KEEP. The general five-probability algorithm is not obtained.\n\nUse the standard Z2 sandpile convention: a site of height at least 4 topples by losing 4 and sending one chip to each of its four neighbours. A stable configuration has heights at most 3. Under any fair legal schedule, every deterministic configuration s>=3 with at least one site of height 4 is nonstabilising, and every site topples infinitely often.\n\nProof: if every site toppled only finitely often, its finite nonnegative integer odometer u would satisfy s+Delta u<=3, with Delta u(x)=sum_{y~x}u(y)-4u(x). Thus Delta u<=0 everywhere and Delta u<=-1 at a height-4 site. Every finite nonnegative superharmonic function on recurrent Z2 is constant: for simple random walk started at x and stopped on hitting y or at n, superharmonicity and nonnegativity give u(x)>=u(y) P_x(T_y<=n); recurrence makes this probability tend to 1. Interchanging x and y proves equality. Constancy contradicts the strict inequality at the seed. Hence some site must topple infinitely. Infinite topplings send infinitely many chips to every neighbour, and fairness then forces that neighbour to topple infinitely; connectedness propagates this to all sites.\n\nFor iid heights supported on {3,4}, if P(s=4)=0, the field is identically 3 and is already stable. If P(s=4)>0, almost surely there is a height-4 site (indeed infinitely many), so the preceding deterministic argument gives probability one of infinite toppling at every site. For rationally specified probabilities this gives a finite algorithm on that face by testing P(4)=0 exactly. The probability classification holds for arbitrary real probabilities; we do not claim a decidable zero test for an unspecified real-number encoding. Every law supported on {0,1,2,3} is also already stable.\n\nIn particular the stable background s=3 plus one