+      "title": "A Sierpinski-carpet boundary remainder contains a nontrivial segment",
+      "statement": "Let K be the standard Sierpinski carpet in the unit square. Remove the boundaries of all connected components of R^2 minus K. The remaining set contains {(1/4,y):0<y<1}, hence is not totally disconnected, although K is compact, connected and has area zero. The number 1/4 has ternary expansion 0.020202..., lies in the Cantor set and is not a gap endpoint; every deleted-square horizontal projection is contained in a closed Cantor gap, so the segment avoids all hole boundaries and the outer boundary. This deterministic set is not claimed to be a Brownian trace.",
+      "proof_text": "# P0021: boundary deletion need not disconnect a general planar zero-area continuum\n\nLabel: proof of a deterministic obstruction to a generic topological route, pending w347 KEEP. This is not a Brownian-trace counterexample.\n\nLet K be the standard Sierpinski carpet in [0,1]^2, obtained by repeatedly deleting the open middle square from each retained square. It is compact and connected and has area zero, since the area of the stage-n approximant is (8/9)^n. Its complement has the deleted open squares as bounded connected components and the exterior of the unit square as its unbounded component.\n\nTake x=1/4. Its ternary expansion is 0.020202...; therefore it belongs to the middle-third Cantor set and is not an endpoint of a complementary interval. The horizontal projection of every closed deleted square is contained in a closed middle-third gap from a finite stage, so none contains x. Consequently every point (1/4,y) with 0<y<1 lies in K and on none of the boundaries of any complementary component. The exterior component's boundary is the perimeter of the unit square and also misses this open vertical segment. After deleting all component boundaries, K therefore retains a connected nontrivial segment and is not totally disconnected.\n\nZero area, empty interior and connectedness of the original planar continuum cannot suffice to prove the Brownian assertion. Brownian-specific geometry must rule out this mechanism. The earlier time-set argument remains invalid too: a continuous image of a totally disconnected compact set can be connected (the Cantor-function example). Neither route settles the spatial remainder. Numerical controls below check the exact x=1/4 witness against the first ten Cantor deletion stages; the ternary expansion proves the claim at every stage.\n\nSource: existing P0021 problem and attempt cards, citing Burdzy, My favorite open problems, Problem 2(ii). This example is an elementary topological control, with no novelty claim.\n",
